      </button>

   </div>
</header>
  <main class="container">
<div class="aeo-authority-block">
        <a href="/districts/nyc-district-06-washington-heights/">← Back to District 6 Hub</a>
    </div>
    
    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 6 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District District 6. Navigating the special education system can be challenging, so this directory aims to provide you with the correct points of contact and actionable strategies to advocate effectively for your child's needs. Remember, you are your child's strongest advocate, and understanding your rights and the proper channels of communication is crucial.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 10 (Serving District 6)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>388 West 125th Street, New York, NY 10027</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:212-342-8300">212-342-8300</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Delays in response can significantly impact timelines for evaluations and service provision, so prompt escalation is key. Maintain meticulous records of all communication attempts, including dates, times, methods of contact (phone, email), and the names of individuals contacted.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window mandated by NYS Part 200.5(a)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>. This is a critical point to understand to ensure your child receives timely evaluations and services.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary. This creates a written record of your conversation and confirms your understanding of any agreements made.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em>. This ensures that your email is properly categorized and prioritized by the recipient.</li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names. A simple spreadsheet or notebook can be invaluable for tracking your communication with the district. Include the date, time, method of communication, the person you spoke with, and a brief summary of the conversation.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call. Be clear and concise in your communication. State your request clearly and provide all necessary information.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal). Copying the principal demonstrates that you are taking the matter seriously and ensures that they are aware of the situation.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. Refer to your previous attempts to contact the CSE and clearly state that you have not received a response. Explain the impact of the delay on your child's education.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or timelines are not met. According to IDEA (Individuals with Disabilities Education Act) and NYS Part 200 Regulations, parents have the right to file a formal complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED) if they believe the school district has violated their child's rights under special education law.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is the Committee on Special Education (CSE)?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">The Committee on Special Education (CSE) is a multidisciplinary team responsible for determining a student's eligibility for special education services and developing an Individualized Education Program (IEP) as mandated by IDEA and NYS Part 200. The CSE includes parents, special education teachers, general education teachers, a school psychologist, and a district representative. You, as the parent, are a critical member of this team, and your input is essential.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is an IEP and how is it developed?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">An Individualized Education Program (IEP) is a legally binding document that outlines a student's specific educational needs, goals, and the services required to meet those needs, as per NYS Part 200.4(d)(2). The IEP is developed collaboratively by the CSE team, including parents. The IEP must include measurable annual goals, a statement of how the student's progress will be measured, and a description of the special education and related services that will be provided. Parents have the right to participate in all IEP meetings and to disagree with any part of the IEP.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What if I disagree with the CSE's recommendations?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">If you disagree with the CSE's recommendations, you have several options, as outlined in IDEA and NYS Part 200.5(k). You can request another meeting to discuss your concerns, seek mediation with an impartial mediator, or file a due process complaint. Filing a due process complaint initiates a formal legal process where an impartial hearing officer will make a decision regarding the dispute. It's often advisable to seek legal counsel from a special education attorney before filing a due process complaint.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What are my rights as a parent in the special education process?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">Parents have numerous rights throughout the special education process, as guaranteed by IDEA and NYS Part 200. These rights include the right to participate in all meetings regarding your child's special education, the right to access your child's educational records, the right to obtain an independent educational evaluation (IEE) at public expense if you disagree with the school's evaluation, and the right to file a complaint with NYSED if you believe the school district has violated your child's rights.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">What is an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE)?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">An Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) is an evaluation of your child conducted by a qualified professional who is not employed by the school district. Under IDEA and NYS Part 200.5(g), you have the right to request an IEE at public expense if you disagree with the school district's evaluation of your child. The school district must either pay for the IEE or initiate a due process hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate. IEEs can provide valuable insights and support your advocacy efforts.</p>
    </div>

</section>

<section class="advocacy-tips-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-bullhorn"></i> Parent Advocacy Tips for District 6
    </h2>

    <div class="advocacy-tip" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Tip #1: Know Your Child's Rights</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">Familiarize yourself with the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State Part 200 Regulations. Understanding your child's rights is the first step in effective advocacy. NYSED provides numerous resources on its website, including publications on parental rights in special education.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="advocacy-tip" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Tip #2: Build a Strong Paper Trail</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">Document everything. Keep copies of all correspondence, evaluations, IEPs, and meeting notes. This documentation will be invaluable if you need to escalate your concerns or file a complaint. Use a consistent system for organizing your documents so you can easily access them when needed.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="advocacy-tip" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Tip #3: Collaborate with the School Team</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">While it's important to advocate for your child's needs, try to maintain a collaborative relationship with the school team. Approach meetings with a problem-solving mindset and be open to considering different perspectives. Remember, the goal is to work together to create the best possible educational plan for your child.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="advocacy-tip" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Tip #4: Don't Be Afraid to Ask Questions</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">If you don't understand something, ask for clarification. Special education jargon can be confusing, so don't hesitate to ask the school team to explain things in plain language. It's better to ask questions than to make assumptions or misunderstand important information.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="advocacy-tip" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #333; margin-bottom: 5px;">Tip #5: Seek Support from Other Parents</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">Connect with other parents of children with disabilities in District 6. Sharing experiences and resources can be incredibly helpful. Parent support groups can provide emotional support, practical advice, and valuable insights into navigating the special education system.</p>
    </div>

</section>
    </div>
</main>
